# Information batch v1: ISBLANK ISNUMBER ISTEXT ISERROR NA TYPE — the ONE error-TRANSPARENT family.
# EXPECTED values are authored INDEPENDENTLY of FSA1 by a standalone python3 oracle that models
# the family's semantics from scratch (it INSPECTS an operand's kind and reports on it; it never
# propagates an error/blank/array through it). See PROVENANCE.md ("Information batch"). The oracle's
# authoring run printed:
#
#   ISBLANK(empty)=TRUE  ISBLANK(5)=FALSE  ISBLANK("")=FALSE
#   ISNUMBER(42)=TRUE    ISNUMBER("42")=FALSE   ISNUMBER(1/0)=FALSE
#   ISTEXT("hi")=TRUE    ISTEXT(3)=FALSE
#   ISERROR(1/0)=TRUE    ISERROR(NA())=TRUE     ISERROR(10)=FALSE
#   NA()=#N/A            NA()+1=#N/A (error propagates through arithmetic, OUTSIDE the info fn)
#   TYPE(7)=1  TYPE("x")=2  TYPE(TRUE)=4  TYPE(NA())=16  TYPE(empty)=1  TYPE({1,2})=64
#
# The defining contract this batch proves: an info predicate is error-TRANSPARENT — ISERROR(1/0) is
# TRUE (the #DIV/0! is CAUGHT, not returned) and TYPE(NA()) is 16 (not #N/A). Contrast NA()+1, where
# the same #N/A DOES propagate because arithmetic (not an info fn) is doing the reading. An ARRAY
# operand is inspected AS an array: TYPE(A1:B1)=64, and the IS-predicates see "not a scalar of my
# kind" -> FALSE, never a scalarize-into-#VALUE!. The parser has no {..} array-constant node in v1,
# so the array operand is expressed as a range reference A1:B1 (which evaluates to a 1x2 array).

# --- ISBLANK: TRUE only for a genuinely empty cell; an empty STRING is not blank (the edge). ---

[isblank-empty-cell]
funcs: ISBLANK
note: A1 is undefined in the context -> the resolver reads it as Blank -> ISBLANK is TRUE.
formula: =ISBLANK(A1)
expect: TRUE

[isblank-has-number]
funcs: ISBLANK
cell A1: 5
formula: =ISBLANK(A1)
expect: FALSE

[isblank-empty-string-is-not-blank]
funcs: ISBLANK
note: an empty string "" is a text VALUE, not an empty cell -> FALSE (the classic ISBLANK edge).
cell A1: ""
formula: =ISBLANK(A1)
expect: FALSE

# --- ISNUMBER: TRUE only for a real number; numeric-looking TEXT is not a number; an error operand
# is inspected (not propagated) -> FALSE. ---

[isnumber-number]
funcs: ISNUMBER
cell A1: 42
formula: =ISNUMBER(A1)
expect: TRUE

[isnumber-numeric-text-is-false]
funcs: ISNUMBER
note: "42" is text, not a number -> FALSE (ISNUMBER does not coerce).
cell A1: "42"
formula: =ISNUMBER(A1)
expect: FALSE

[isnumber-error-operand-is-false]
funcs: ISNUMBER
note: error-transparent edge — 1/0 evaluates to #DIV/0!, which is NOT a number -> FALSE (the error
note: is inspected, never propagated).
formula: =ISNUMBER(1/0)
expect: FALSE

# --- ISTEXT: TRUE only for text. ---

[istext-text]
funcs: ISTEXT
cell A1: "hi"
formula: =ISTEXT(A1)
expect: TRUE

[istext-number-is-false]
funcs: ISTEXT
cell A1: 3
formula: =ISTEXT(A1)
expect: FALSE

# --- ISERROR: the defining error-TRANSPARENT case — an operand that evaluates to ANY error (incl.
# #N/A) is CAUGHT and reported TRUE, never returned as the error itself. ---

[iserror-catches-divzero]
funcs: ISERROR
note: 1/0 -> #DIV/0!; ISERROR CATCHES it -> TRUE (not #DIV/0!). This is the family's whole point.
formula: =ISERROR(1/0)
expect: TRUE

[iserror-catches-na]
funcs: ISERROR, NA
note: ISERROR catches #N/A too (unlike ISERR, which is deferred).
formula: =ISERROR(NA())
expect: TRUE

[iserror-clean-value-is-false]
funcs: ISERROR
cell A1: 10
formula: =ISERROR(A1)
expect: FALSE

# --- NA: mints #N/A on demand (arity 0). Outside an info fn, that #N/A propagates normally. ---

[na-produces-na]
funcs: NA
formula: =NA()
expect: #N/A

[na-propagates-through-arithmetic]
funcs: NA
note: edge — NA() is the sole error-PRODUCING member; here the #N/A rides through arithmetic (NOT an
note: info fn), so it PROPAGATES, in contrast to ISERROR(NA())=TRUE where an info fn catches it.
formula: =NA()+1
expect: #N/A

# --- TYPE: the Excel type code 1/2/4/16/64; error and array operands are transparent (16 / 64). ---

[type-of-number]
funcs: TYPE
cell A1: 7
formula: =TYPE(A1)
expect: 1

[type-of-text]
funcs: TYPE
cell A1: "x"
formula: =TYPE(A1)
expect: 2

[type-of-logical]
funcs: TYPE
formula: =TYPE(TRUE)
expect: 4

[type-of-error-is-16]
funcs: TYPE, NA
note: error-transparent edge — a #N/A operand reports code 16, it is NOT returned as #N/A.
formula: =TYPE(NA())
expect: 16

[type-of-array-is-64]
funcs: TYPE
note: a multi-cell range evaluates to an array operand -> code 64 (inspected AS an array, never
note: scalarized to #VALUE!). Expressed as A1:B1 since v1 has no {..} array-constant node.
cell A1: 1
cell B1: 2
formula: =TYPE(A1:B1)
expect: 64
